- F-145 **Walk refuses at load an object that leaves an inherited abstract method uncovered.** Bodies at narrower parameter types define an abstract method only where together they cover its parameter types, with `comprises` clauses read, as P-147 decides: each position is split through the clauses, and every combination of parts must lie inside one body. A search that does not settle refuses the object: a cycle, a symbolic type or a type that the search cannot read, 10,000 steps or 4,096 combinations. A generic object is not checked (row 665). An object whose one covering definition has a static parameter of its own is refused, though it is valid (row 728, a defect of walk; the compiled path runs it). The compiled checker covers the same way, within the same bounds, except that where the abstract method or a body below it has static parameters of its own, it splits nothing and refuses the object (row 725). Walk's `override` check at load is F-147's, and its check of the Meet Rule for Functional Methods is F-142's. Gated by `tests/AbstractMethod*Walk` (`AbstractMethodCoverUnsettledWalk` among them) and `compiler_tests/AbstractMethodCoveredPairs`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-coverage/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 6 (the cover); `compile-ladder/rung-duplicates-coverage/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 8 (the refusal of a search that does not settle).
  by: the clarity pass; note: reorganized (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f13/proposals.md, "F-145"); the title narrowed to coverage, its one subject, since F-147 owns the override check and F-142 the meet check; the OverrideNothing*Walk gate moved to F-147, whose Override*Walk glob holds it; each report's part named, rung-walk-coverage's sections 1 and 6 still saying that an unsettled search does not refuse; fix round: the hand-over no longer claims the two as the only other checks (references/interpreter.md:36-43 lists more); "unreadable type" said as the report says it (rung-duplicates-coverage/REPORT.md:48); the checker's behaviour at row 725 and row 728's class, a defect of walk, said from the rows
- F-146 **The compiled checker accepts arithmetic in a size, and compares two sizes by their written form once it has computed each operation on numerals.** It builds P-55, the curator's decision on arithmetic in a size. Two sizes are the same where their written forms match once each operation on numerals is computed. They differ only where they are two different numerals or two different size parameters, so the checker calls a `typecase` clause unreachable that walk reaches at equal arguments (row 683, contested). Any other pair, such as a size name against a numeral, or two operations written differently, is not known to differ. Neither path infers a static parameter that a parameter type writes inside an operation (row 767). The compiled class loader can make a size outside its kind's range, as a `nat` above 4294967295, where walk refuses it (row 675). Gated by `compiler_tests/NatArith*`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-size-expressions/REPORT.md` section 1, "Item 15, 15a with its part (b)", and section 12.
  by: the clarity pass; note: reorganized (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f13/proposals.md, "F-146"); split: the opening of where-clause sizes, a default under review, is now F-?1; the title's second half from P-55 and the report's section 1 (nEq); rows 683, 767 and 675 given their subjects from their ledger cells; row 682 and row 307 moved with the opening; fix round: the comparison's three answers said as pEqv gives them (the same, different, not known to differ); "beyond its kind" said as a nat above 4294967295 (row 675)
- F-?1 **The compiled checker opens a value of a trait whose `comprises` clause lists a type at a `where`-clause size: it gives each listed type's `nat` and `int` `where`-clause sizes a fresh name for the call.** In `trait NatParam comprises { N[\n\] } where [\ nat n \]`, a `NatParam` passed where `N[\$n\]` is expected, `$n` being the checker's inference variable for the called function's size parameter, binds `$n` to the fresh size, and the call's result type carries it. An opened size is not known to differ from any size. Walk's `comprises` check at load reads such a size as any size, the same at each of its places; at a call, walk binds the argument's own size. This is a default, way 4a of fork 3 (a size known only at run time) in PLAN's section "Pavol's answers, in the order they are needed", item 13. An agent landed it, and it waits for the curator's review (review-queue item 30); P-85, his position that the array questions are open, records it as the one exception. The code generator does not compile such a trait: "Can't compile TraitDecl NatParamT" (row 307; `compiler_tests/XXXNatOpenRun`). In row 682's program, with `trait NatParamT comprises { Nt[\n\] } where [\nat n\]`, the checker refuses `f(x: NatParamT)` beside `f[\nat n\](x: Nt[\n\])` as two declarations of one parameter type, where walk runs the pair (row 682, contested). F-251 holds the same opening for a type variable. Gated by `compiler_tests/NatOpenChecker`, and under walk by `tests/NatOpenWalk` and `NatOpenUnlistedExtender`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-size-expressions/REPORT.md` section 1, "Fork 3, way 4a, under Q13.3", and section 12.
