- F-147 **Walk refuses an `override` that overrides nothing; an inherited declaration is overridden where each of its parameter types is a subtype of the `override`'s and not all are equal.** This is the traits chapter's "strict subtype" (`Specification/basic/traits.tex:585-589`). At an instance of a generic trait that an object without static parameters extends, equal parameter types still count as overridden (`OverrideGenericInstanceEqualTypesWalk`). Walk checks the `override` of a generic trait, object or object expression once, at its declaration. An object inherits nothing that a trait's `override` overrides, so an object below an abstract `override` that declares no body is refused at load (rows 614, 649; `AbstractOverrideUndefinedWalk`, "does not define an abstract method declared in type W"). Walk does not read all the bounds of the static parameters in an object expression, which takes them from the function around it, or in a generic trait or object that extends a type under a `where` clause. There it accepts an `override` over an inherited declaration whose parameter types mention a static parameter and differ from the `override`'s (row 693). The `fortress-repo` skill gives the rule's cases (`references/interpreter.md`, "What walk checks"). Gated by `tests/Override*Walk.fss` and `AbstractOverrideUndefinedWalk`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-override/REPORT.md` section 1.
  by: the clarity pass; note: reworded (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f13/proposals.md, "F-147"); "the chapter's strict subtype" stated as the rule (rung-walk-override/REPORT.md section 1); the row 693 clause limited to types that differ from the override's, and its two cases named, as row 693 and the skill say; AbstractOverrideUndefinedWalk (row 649) added as a gate; fix round: the title's "only" dropped and the equal-types exception at a generic trait's instance stated (Constructor.java:588, :595; OverrideGenericInstanceEqualTypesWalk); "with static parameters" attached to the type; what follows an abstract override said, a refusal at load
- F-228 **Under walk, a reduction or a comprehension that writes no static argument instantiates its big operator by a declared type, else by the join of its elements' run-time types, else at its bound, where an F-bounded parameter stays open.** It builds P-48. The declared type is a local variable's, or a top-level or local function's declared return type, for either also where the clause form ends a block. A method's return type, a top-level variable, a field, an assignment, a function expression, a branch, a call's argument and another clause form's body do not direct it. Otherwise the clauses run once into the library's collector, `__elemsOf`, which keeps the elements in order, typed at the join of their run-time types, and the operator is instantiated at that join; the reduction then runs over the collected elements, so each body runs once. A declared type or a join that does not fit falls back to the instance that no hint gives: a plain-bounded parameter takes its bound, and an F-bounded one stays open (F-143). So a `SUM` of `ZZ32` and `RR64` values, whose join `Number` is outside the bound, runs with each `+` at its operands' types. The same holds with no element and no declared type. With the big operator so instantiated, the library types `__bigOperator`'s local function `body(i: I)` at the element type `I`. A big operator whose declared result is a trait of its own is read through the `BigReduction`, `Comprehension` or `BigOperator` that the trait extends (row 718, fixed). Still wrong, each a ledger row: an empty clause form that no declared type directs takes its bound or stays open (row 716); a comprehension that no declared type reaches is built at its elements' run-time class, as `ArrayList[\Int\]`, which a declared `List[\ZZ32\]` refuses (rows 20, 717); a clause form that is a call's argument gets no declared type (rows 510, 705). Gated by `tests/UnwrittenListMapComprehensionWalk`, `UnwrittenComprehension*Walk`, `ComprehensionTypedBindingWalk`, `UnwrittenSetBigOperatorsWalk`, `UnwrittenEmptyFloatSumWalk`, `IntMapComprehensionTypedWalk` and `UserBigOperatorResultTraitWalk`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-elements/REPORT.md` sections 1, 5 (5.3 lists the contexts) and 10, decision 6; `compile-ladder/rung-walk-overload-if/REPORT.md` section 1, "Row 718".
  by: the clarity pass; note: reorganized (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f13/proposals.md, "F-228"); the third step given both cases, a plain bound and F-143's open parameter (rung-walk-elements/REPORT.md section 1, item 3); the title's "the declared type of its value" narrowed to the contexts reached, with those not reached from section 5.3; moved here: "by the coordinator's call (the walk probe's way 6 + 7), and lifts D2", ways 6 and 7 of perf-probes/walk-plain-bound/REPORT.md and decision D2, a plain-bounded parameter kept at BottomType; the collector's lines Library/FortressLibrary.fss:1318-1437 (now to :1439), EvaluatorBase.instantiateBigOperator and the per-argument caches, EvaluatorBase.libraryBigResult; fix round: the fallback of a declared type or join that does not fit added (section 1; section 10, decision 6; the Number example from the skill's revival-changes.md, "Static parameters"); the typed body tied back to the instantiation, not to row 718's repair (Library/FortressLibrary.fss:1314); "Left open" renamed, so that it does not read as the open type, with what goes wrong in each row; the collector and the block tail of both contexts said
- F-238 **Under walk's load check, an exclusion that two declarations with static parameters of their own show only when each is read at a symbolic instantiation is withdrawn where their instances do not exclude.** A declaration with static parameters of its own is defined in F-252. The check first reads two generic functions of one name and one list of static parameters at one shared symbolic instantiation, and two instances of one generic object as two object types that cannot be extended, so they exclude. So `o[\T\](x: P[\T\])` beside `o[\T\](x: Q[\T\])`, with `trait P[\T\] excludes { Q[\T\] }`, excludes at one `T`, though a type that extends `P[\ZZ32\]` and `Q[\String\]` has both. Walk then reads such an exclusion again over the instances of each. Instances of one generic type exclude only where their static arguments cannot be equal. Any other exclusion stands where the two still exclude with their static parameters renamed apart. Or, read at the shared instantiation, it stands where every generic type that both extend forces their static arguments to be equal, since no type extends two instances of one generic trait: so `Array` and `ImmutableArray` exclude at every pair of instances through `ReadableArray`. A pair whose exclusion is withdrawn goes on to the reading on declared domains (F-252), and a refusal there names the rule that the pair breaks (F-257). Gated, as refusals at load, by `tests/GenericBoundsNoMeetWalk`, `NoDuplicatesRuleWalk`, `NoDuplicatesRuleSizeWalk` and `GenericOverloadInstancesApartWalk`; as programs that load, by `GenericBoundsMeetWalk`, `GenericFBoundsMeetWalk`, `GenericPairExcludingWalk`, `GenericSizePairNestedWalk`, `ShuffleTest` and `GenericPairExcludesTogetherWalk`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-overload-if/REPORT.md` section 1, "Row 373", and section 9.
